meta {
  name: 07-session-lifecycle
  seq: 8
}

docs {
  A session from login to logout, driven as its own isolated session so nothing
  here disturbs the `owner` tokens the rest of the collection depends on.

  This folder exists for a class of bug the unit suite structurally cannot
  reach: **state that is individually correct but wrong in sequence.** Every
  endpoint involved passes its own test. What breaks is the composition —

  - a refresh token that still works after logout,
  - a rotated token whose predecessor stays valid, so a stolen one never expires,
  - a logout that revokes one session and silently ends all of them.

  Each is only visible across several requests that share accumulated state,
  which is exactly what this suite is for and what a service-level test would
  have to hand-build (and could then hand-build wrongly).

  **On access tokens after logout.** Logout revokes the *refresh* token; a JWT
  access token stays valid until it expires, because it is verified by signature
  with no database lookup. That is a deliberate trade — stateless verification
  in exchange for a short window where a logged-out access token still works —
  and request 05 pins it as intended behavior rather than leaving the next
  reader to discover it and file a bug.
}
